==Old Norse==

===Etymology===
{{root|non|ine-pro|*sed-}}
Causative of {{m|non|sitja}}. From {{inh|non|gmq-pro|ᛊᚨᛏᛁᛞᛟ}} (''1st singular past indicative''), {{inh|non|gem-pro|*satjaną}}, from {{inh|non|ine-pro|*sodéyeti}}.

===Verb===
{{non-verb}}

# {{lb|non|transitive}} to [[seat]], [[set]], [[place]], [[put]]
# to [[drive]] {{+obj|non|acc}}
#: {{ux|non|hann setti øxina í hǫfuð honum|he drove the axe into his (not his own) skull}}
# to [[make]], [[establish]] {{+obj|non|acc}}
#: {{ux|non|Þeir vildu setja grið í milli konungs ok bonda|they wanted to make peace between the king and the peasants}}
# to [[order]], [[prescribe]] {{+obj|non|dat}}
# to [[settle]] {{+obj|non|dat}}
# {{lb|non|intransitive}} to [[set off]]

====Conjugation====
Note that ''sett-'' in the past indicative is a specifically West Norse (Icelandic, Norwegian Nynorsk and Faroese) innovation; East Norse (Swedish, Danish and Norwegian Bokmål) retains original ''satt-''.
{{non-conj-weak1-short|set|1s=sez|sett}}

